"""Thirty original first-week MCQs and eight original Mains frameworks."""
import json,pathlib,random,re
R=pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];assert not (R/'data/2026-11-mcqs.json').exists()
rows='''CA-043~How should the 5 November tax release be classified?~An early release of the regular November instalment~A new discretionary grant outside regular devolution|A new horizontal-sharing formula|Proof all states had already spent the amount~The primary release advances the normally 10 November instalment of ₹1,09,019 crore to 5 November. Timing changes, not a verified additional entitlement.
CA-043~Which AP/Telangana devolution pairing is correct?~AP ₹4,597 crore; Telangana ₹2,370 crore~AP ₹2,370 crore; Telangana ₹4,597 crore|AP ₹1,09,019 crore; Telangana nil|Both states receive identical ₹4,597 crore~The inspected state table distinguishes the two states. A union release is separate from subsequent departmental allotment and actual expenditure.
1~Which distinguishes the proposed 8th Economic Census correctly?~It concerns non-farm establishments, not a population headcount~It is the eighth Census of all people in independent India|It has already published every district’s revised GDP|It automatically replaces every sample survey~VISTAR’s Economic Census component supports establishment-level information and a statistical business register. Planned surveys and data architecture are not completed results.
1~What is VISTAR’s approved outlay and scheme type?~₹4,371.10 crore; central-sector scheme~₹3,788.04 crore; exclusively state-funded scheme|₹14,500 crore; universal individual cash transfer|₹2,814 crore; already completed Economic Census~The 4 November release gives the precise outlay for 2026–31. State statistical strengthening is one component, but this does not turn the programme into the distinct FTSC centrally sponsored scheme.
2~Which pairing correctly distinguishes existing and proposed FTSC capacity?~779 functional courts; provision to expand to 850~850 existing; expansion to 779|850 new courts in every state|779 exclusive POCSO courts in each district~The release’s existing functional total includes 399 exclusive POCSO courts. The 850 target is total proposed scheme capacity, not additional courts or proof of filled posts.
2~How is the FTSC continuation financed?~Central and state/UT shares, with the central share from Nirbhaya Fund~Exclusively by every victim’s court fee|Entirely by a new levy on all schools|Only by private insurance companies~The ₹3,788.04 crore total comprises central ₹2,353.15 crore and state/UT ₹1,434.89 crore. Funding, functional staffing and quality outcomes require separate evaluation.
3~What is the planned processing-plant mix under critical-mineral capex support?~Two lithium and two nickel plants~Four lithium mines and no processing plants|Two cobalt-only mines and two graphite refineries|Four operating plants already delivering cells~The scheme targets processing rather than merely mine allocation. Cobalt may arise as a nickel by-product; eligible plant support is not a claim of completed commissioning.
3~Which preserves the capex-support limits?~30% of eligible investment, capped at ₹500 crore for lithium and ₹900 crore for nickel per beneficiary~A uniform guaranteed ₹2,814 crore for each applicant|100% of every cost without milestones|₹500 crore for nickel and ₹900 crore for lithium~Technical qualification and least-support financial bidding precede selection, and disbursement is milestone-linked. Caps are not automatic entitlements or total private investment.
4~Which storage mix totals the approved 92 GWh target?~60 GWh pumped storage + 30 GWh BESS + 2 GWh others~60 GW batteries + 30 GW solar + 2 GW coal|92 GWh already commissioned on approval day|30 GWh pumped storage + 60 GWh BESS + 20 GWh others~GWh measures stored energy, not power. The release explicitly describes yet-to-be-commissioned capacity and distinguishes ₹14,500 crore support from estimated ₹97,600 crore investment.
4~Which domestic-content condition is explicitly described for BESS?~A minimum 40% domestic-content requirement~A universal 100% local-content certificate already issued|No domestic-content requirement for any project|A requirement that 40% of every state’s electricity be stored~The requirement attaches to BESS projects under the described scheme; it is not proof of compliance, an entire grid’s energy share or universal project commissioning.
5~What is the sound legal interpretation of the PAC conference resolution?~A collective oversight commitment, not automatic amendment of every legislature’s rules~A transfer of the CAG’s constitutional role to private auditors|Proof every state PAC now meets three times monthly|An automatic final conviction for every audit observation~The resolution seeks action-taken timelines, digital monitoring, capacity building and consideration of meeting frequency. Audit, committee scrutiny, executive response and legal responsibility differ.
6~How does the Network Anubhav release describe P80 for mobile speed?~80% of tests were at or above the stated speed~80% of every subscriber’s bill is refunded|Exactly 80% of every user’s data is private|It guarantees a minimum speed to every household~The source uses an exceedance convention and warns that user-generated tests are not comprehensive standard-method quality assessment. Do not silently substitute a different percentile convention or population denominator.
7~Which preserves the human-assistance distinction in telecom grievance support?~Round-the-clock support, with human help at least 16 hours daily~Human staffing exactly 24 hours under every circumstance|Only 16 hours of any support, with no other channel|All appeals decided exclusively by unsupervised AI~The release permits supervised technology-based solutions, accessible channels and oversight. The filing and decision timelines are separate from staffing requirements; the full regulation’s operative details still require checking.
7~Which pair of appeal periods is described in the release?~Filing within 15 days after disposal; decision within 15 days of receipt~Filing within 30 years; decision without a deadline|Filing and decision both fixed at one hour|Only a quarterly window to file any appeal~The described filing window changes from 30 to 15 days; the internal appellate authority’s decision period is separately measured from receipt. This note does not independently certify every exception/commencement clause.
8~What remains necessary during international Digi Yatra trials?~Travel documents and mandatory airline/immigration formalities~Only a selfie, with no passport or visa requirements|Universal use at every AP airport|An automatic right to enter any foreign country~Trials cover eligible Indian e-passport holders on selected flights at seven named airports. A device credential complements, not replaces, lawful travel procedures.
9~Which is correct about VAJRA-SAT and FMD SAT-1?~It was preparedness; the release says SAT-1 had not been reported in India~It declared a confirmed SAT-1 outbreak in every state|It concerned human hand-foot-and-mouth disease only|It certified every animal in India disease-free~The 4–5 November tabletop exercise rehearses detection, laboratory confirmation and coordinated response. Simulation, serotype status and actual outbreak evidence must not be conflated.
10~What does SUVIKALP’s TOPSIS output establish?~A ranking based on the chosen criteria and inputs~An automatic environmental clearance|Proof every abandoned mine has been restored|A new title deed for every affected resident~Decision support requires transparent weights, site evidence and community participation. Feasibility, permission, finance, implementation and remediation outcomes remain separate stages.
11~Which condition survives in the Agniveer pathway announcement?~Willingness and meeting selection criteria for later regular enrolment~Unconditional immediate regular appointment for everyone|Abolition of the initial merit-based 25% pathway|No additional engagement period for the remaining group~The 4 November release retains the initial merit-based regular intake and provides a willing remaining group another two years, followed by a conditional regular-enrolment pathway. Service orders remain necessary for individual terms.
12~Which shipbuilder allocation matches the corvette contracts?~GRSE five; GSL three~GRSE three; GSL five|Eight ships all already delivered by Visakhapatnam yard|Five submarines and three carriers~The ₹53,866.50 crore contracts were signed on 2 November. This is beyond AoN but not construction completion, delivery or commissioning; forecast person-days are not permanent job counts.
13~What does the Pralay release establish?~A successful user-evaluation test, with induction described as imminent~Completed combat deployment in every command|A cruise-missile test at an AP launch site|An independent audit of performance under every condition~The test took place off Odisha on 5 November, with RCI Hyderabad and BDL roles described. The note does not invent ranges/warheads or convert imminent induction into completed deployment.
14~What follows from I-OECM recognition?~It does not itself change ownership or create a protected area~Every recognised site automatically becomes a national park|Every private title is automatically extinguished|Every parcel in a demonstration landscape has already been recognised~The guidelines’ release distinguishes sustained biodiversity outcomes, existing governance, application review and monitoring. Srisailam–Cumbum’s landscape inclusion is not blanket parcel-level designation.
15~What is the temporal caution for anti-cancer prices?~The 7 November update states revised prices take effect on 9 November~All future price effects were already audited by 7 November|The update cancels every clinical prescription|The highest reported brand reduction applies equally to every drug~The underlying October NPPA order is discussed through a 7 November release, with 9 November effect. Projected savings and reported formulation MRPs are not universal realised treatment-cost reductions.
16~Which correctly distinguishes the 6 November electronics announcements?~Semicon portal pillars 2/3/4 and 12 additional ECMS approvals are separate measures~All 12 ECMS applicants are already operating semiconductor fabs|Every listed new ECMS state is Andhra Pradesh|Projected investment equals audited government spending~The new ECMS batch has projected ₹7,503 crore investment and seven listed states excluding AP. Portal access, approval, commissioning and output remain distinct.
17~Which is a valid caution about EKA-1 and Dhīr?~Parameter/language claims and offline capability do not prove safety or factual accuracy~On-device deployment makes hallucination impossible|A ministry release is an independent audit of all licences and benchmarks|Every model supports exactly the same languages and architecture~The release describes different developers, sizes and language coverage. Model cards, weights, licence and actual Telugu/task performance require independent evaluation.
18~Which avoids overclaiming BIMSTEC participation?~Seven members do not imply seven navies each sent a ship~BIMSTEC contains only three countries|Landlocked countries cannot belong to BIMSTEC|The exercise creates an automatic collective-defence treaty~The 6 November release lists ships and delegations separately for the 30 October–3 November event. Regional cooperation, participation and treaty obligations differ.
19~What did Tiger Triumph’s inspected conclusion release describe?~India–US tri-service HADR staff/tabletop work, ending at Visakhapatnam on 5 November~An independently confirmed joint combat operation in AP|Proof foreign forces conducted the state’s Murasu rescue|A new exercise with no pre-2026 history~The fifth edition ran 31 October–5 November; the release was dated 6 November. Planning/simulation and an actual disaster deployment are not the same activity.
20~What does the ₹218 crore RINL figure represent in the inspected release?~Successful Phase-II bids for 114 land parcels~Independently audited final cash receipts for the entire steel plant|A state pension allocation for 114 households|A verified third-round auction already completed~Land monetisation differs from enterprise disinvestment. The combined two-phase bid value is ₹584.33 crore for 264 parcels; payment and transfer were not independently inspected.
21~Why should ₹4,600 crore not be silently assigned to Murasu?~The cited approaching-storm report’s paragraph concerned preceding Arnab damage~It is a universally fixed compensation for each cyclone|It was proved to be Murasu’s final audited loss|Every newspaper’s newest number replaces all earlier records automatically~Consecutive events and evolving assessments need named event, date and attribution. Murasu’s early approximately ₹1,919 crore snapshots also differ slightly and remain preliminary.
22~Which arithmetic matches the reported ₹37 crore emergency allocation?~6×₹3 crore + 8×₹2 crore + 3×₹1 crore~17×₹37 crore|6×₹2 crore + 8×₹3 crore + 3×₹3 crore|₹37 crore of verified central assistance for every district~The district groups total 17, and ₹18+₹16+₹3=₹37 crore. State release, district expenditure and a subsequent central-assistance request are different stages.
24~What is the sound handling of differing horticulture-loss estimates?~Keep source/date snapshots distinct pending verification~Add both reports to double the affected area|Average them into an invented official revision|Treat affected hectares as identical to compensation already paid~IANS’s letter account and TNIE/PTI’s review snapshot give different horticulture figures. Original surveys and final accounts remain unread; area, farmer counts, loss value and entitlements differ.'''
positions=[0]*8+[1]*8+[2]*7+[3]*7;random.Random(18112026).shuffle(positions);Q=[]
for i,row in enumerate(rows.splitlines(),1):
 ref,q,correct,other,ex=row.split('~');opts=other.split('|');random.Random(1800+i).shuffle(opts);pos=positions[i-1];opts.insert(pos,correct);Q.append(dict(id=f'NOV26-{i:02}',question=q,options=opts,answer=pos,explanation=ex,ref=ref if ref.startswith('CA-')else f'E18-2611-{int(ref):03}'))
assert len(Q)==30
(R/'data/2026-11-mcqs.json').write_text(json.dumps(Q,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
F=[
(1,'Official statistical modernisation must combine timeliness with credibility and privacy.',[
'Anchor VISTAR’s ₹4,371.10 crore central-sector approval for 2026–31; distinguish a scheme from completed survey results or newly operative indices.',
'Explain the three components, planned 59 surveys, SSS 2.0 and 8th Economic Census of non-farm establishments; do not confuse it with the population Census.',
'Analyse representativeness, definitions, confidentiality, revisions, administrative/alternative data and transparent methodologies. AI can assist validation but also reproduce systematic errors.',
'For AP, connect trained district statistical teams, independent quality checks and interoperable records with usable local estimates and safeguarded respondent information.',
'Conclude with publication calendars, methodological disclosure, accessible datasets and measurable quality/timeliness, not only dashboards or survey counts.'
]),
(3,'Critical-mineral security requires processing capability as well as access to resources.',[
'Anchor the ₹2,814 crore processing scheme, two lithium and two nickel plants, 30% eligible-capex support and mineral-specific ceilings.',
'Distinguish extraction, refining, precursors, cells, equipment and recycling; importing feedstock can coexist with domestic refining and residual dependencies.',
'Analyse technology, raw-material access, energy, water, waste, skills, offtake and supply concentration; least-support bidding must not displace credible delivery.',
'Propose milestone verification, transparent selection, lifecycle environmental oversight, diversified feedstock and research/industry partnerships.',
'For AP, identify transferable industrial-policy lessons without inventing a local selected beneficiary or treating future plants as commissioned capacity.'
]),
(4,'Storage support should procure useful flexibility rather than merely subsidise capacity headlines.',[
'Anchor ₹14,500 crore VGF and the prospective 92 GWh mix: 60 PSP, 30 BESS and 2 alternatives; distinguish ₹97,600 crore estimated investment.',
'Explain energy versus power, duration, efficiency, degradation, safety, water needs and actual services such as balancing/peak supply.',
'Analyse competitive tariff design, 40% BESS domestic-content verification, project risk, disposal/recycling and transparent public liabilities.',
'For AP DISCOMs, use demand/renewable profiles, available networks and credible availability obligations to choose projects and technology.',
'Conclude with useful delivered flexibility, lifecycle costs and safe operation; no inference that approved GWh are already commissioned or eliminate every supply constraint.'
]),
(2,'Special courts need both faster processes and a child-sensitive, fair justice system.',[
'Anchor the FTSC continuation through March 2031, ₹3,788.04 crore and expansion from 779 functional courts towards 850; distinguish exclusive POCSO courts.',
'Map investigation, forensics, prosecution, legal aid, judicial vacancies, witness support and vulnerable-witness spaces as a complete service chain.',
'Analyse why disposal speed alone can conceal poor investigation, distress, unfair procedure, unreliable evidence or repeated adjournments elsewhere.',
'Propose dedicated competent personnel, accessible legal/psychosocial support, reasoned decisions, coordinated scheduling and independent qualitative assessment.',
'For AP, allocate capacity by pendency and need rather than assuming a fixed uniform court entitlement for every district; preserve fair-trial and child-protection principles.'
]),
(7,'Digital complaint handling should increase accessibility without substituting automation for remedies.',[
'Anchor the 2 November telecom release; distinguish announced final amendments from separately uninspected commencement/exemption clauses.',
'Explain round-the-clock support with at least 16 hours of human help, multiple accessible channels and distinct 15-day filing/decision periods.',
'Analyse language, disability access, digital exclusion, AI errors, wrongful closures and the burden of shorter appeal windows.',
'Use feedback, quarterly oversight, consumer participation and audits to identify recurring defects; preserve human escalation and reasoned responses.',
'For AP, evaluate actual resolution, timeliness and vulnerable consumers’ experience rather than equating app downloads, closed tickets or raw satisfaction scores with success.'
]),
(14,'Conservation beyond protected areas must combine ecological outcomes and legitimate local governance.',[
'Anchor the 3 November I-OECM guideline event, reported on 4 November, and distinguish recognition from a protected-area notification or transfer of ownership.',
'Explain boundaries, non-overlap, institutions, review, sustained in-situ outcomes and monitoring; full primary guideline/project texts were not independently inspected.',
'Analyse local rights, existing uses, financing, consent, biodiversity significance and the danger of nominal or double-counted conservation.',
'For AP, connect the named Srisailam–Cumbum demonstration landscape, local biodiversity institutions, tanks and ecosystems without blanket parcel-level recognition claims.',
'Conclude with transparent decisions, accessible grievance routes and credible indicators, not only recognised-area totals or conservation labels.'
]),
(20,'Public-land monetisation should demonstrate public value beyond auction premiums.',[
'Anchor RINL Phase II on 3 November: 114 parcels and ₹218 crore successful bids; preserve the distinction between bids, settlement and realised receipts.',
'Distinguish surplus-land monetisation from sale of the operating steel enterprise; note Phase I’s October date rather than counting it twice in November.',
'Analyse title, public uses, infrastructure, planning conditions, bidder access, market competition, benchmark valuation and distribution of benefits/costs.',
'Propose transparent surveys/terms, appropriate safeguards, audited settlement and disclosure of how proceeds support lawful public/enterprise purposes.',
'For Visakhapatnam, assess long-term urban and industrial implications; a premium over reserve is evidence of one comparison, not a conclusive welfare assessment.'
]),
(21,'Consecutive cyclones require integrated relief, lifeline restoration and trustworthy loss assessment.',[
'Anchor Murasu’s 3 November reported crossing near Ongole after Arnab; separate forecasts, observed reports, preliminary loss snapshots and final assessments.',
'Map evacuation, shelters, health, food, drinking water, safe electricity, communications, roads and public warning as linked functions.',
'Distinguish ₹37 crore state emergency allocation, regular devolution, private insurance and requested central assistance; none automatically establishes final household receipt.',
'Compare source-specific crop/horticulture estimates and timestamped feeder/tower repair data; do not merge conflicting figures or treat an asset percentage as universal service recovery.',
'For AP, combine accessible verification/appeals and livelihood rehabilitation with resilient siting, drainage, ecosystems and transparent reconstruction expenditure.'
])]
by={n['id']:n for n in json.loads((R/'data/notes.json').read_text())};out=['# 1–7 November 2026 · Original Mains frameworks','','Eight 150/250-word planning frameworks, not previous-year questions. This is a first-week selection, not full November coverage.','']
for i,(ref,q,pts)in enumerate(F,1):
 k=f'E18-2611-{ref:03}';out += [f'## {i}. {q}',f'Study anchor: {k} · {by[k]["title"]}','']+[f'{j}. {t}'for j,t in enumerate(pts,1)]+['']
(R/'volumes/November-2026-Mains-Frameworks.md').write_text('\n'.join(out)+'\n')
print('Authored 30 questions and eight frameworks.')
